---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';
import DataTable from '../../components/DataTable.astro';
import PageHeader from '../../components/PageHeader.astro';

/*
 * The two homepage directions that were NOT implemented, kept as labeled design
 * alternatives. This page is noindex, out of the sitemap, and out of navigation,
 * because shipping three competing homepages would be worse than shipping one.
 */

const trail = [
	{ name: 'Home', path: '/' },
	{ name: 'Design', path: '/design/homepage-concepts' },
];

const SCORES = [
	['Immediate comprehension', '5', '3', '4', 'Can a first-time visitor tell what this is and what to do in one glance?'],
	['Source trust in the first viewport', '4', '5', '3', 'How strongly does the first screen signal that answers carry evidence?'],
	['Mobile usability', '5', '3', '4', 'Does the first screen stay usable at 390px without scrolling past the point?'],
	['Return usefulness', '5', '4', '4', 'Is it fast for someone who already knows what they want?'],
	['Organic search depth', '3', '3', '5', 'How many substantive pages get a real internal link from the homepage?'],
	['Accessibility', '5', '4', '4', 'Heading order, focus order, and reading order without special handling.'],
	['Performance risk', '5', '4', '4', 'Weight and layout stability of the first viewport.'],
	['Freedom from lead-generation pressure', '5', '5', '5', 'All three refuse gating. This was never the differentiator.'],
	['TOTAL', '37', '31', '33', 'Out of 40.'],
];
---

<BaseLayout
	title="Homepage concept comparison"
	description="Internal record of the three homepage directions, how they scored, and why the implemented one won."
	noindex
	pageType="editorial"
>
	<PageHeader
		eyebrow="Internal reference, not a public page"
		title="Homepage concepts: the comparison and the decision"
		lede="Three complete directions were specified against the same identity, tokens, content model, accessibility standard, and evidence rules. They differ only in information hierarchy. One shipped."
		trail={trail}
	/>

	<section class="band band-tight">
		<div class="shell">
			<Callout tone="note">
				<p>
					<strong>Only one homepage exists.</strong> This page is the record of the two that did not
					ship. It is noindex, excluded from the sitemap, and unlinked from navigation. Shipping
					three competing homepages would split the decision rather than make it.
				</p>
			</Callout>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="band band-tight" aria-labelledby="scores-title">
		<div class="shell">
			<h2 id="scores-title">Scoring</h2>
			<p class="lede">
				A: Research Command Center. B: Evidence Ledger. C: Insurance Topic Atlas. Scored 1 to 5 on
				each criterion.
			</p>
			<div style="margin-top: var(--s-5)">
				<DataTable
					caption="Concept scoring. A = Research Command Center, B = Evidence Ledger, C = Insurance Topic Atlas."
					columns={['Criterion', 'A', 'B', 'C', 'What was being judged']}
					rows={SCORES}
				/>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="band band-tight band-paper" aria-labelledby="a-title">
		<div class="shell">
			<span class="eyebrow">Direction A, implemented</span>
			<h2 id="a-title">Research Command Center</h2>
			<div class="prose">
				<p>
					Question first. The first viewport is a working surface: identity and a one-sentence scope
					statement, a prominent question field with a rotating example, four context selects for
					insurance type, line, state, and audience, a browse-by-topic fallback, and a source-quality
					strip counted from the registry rather than asserted.
				</p>
				<p>
					<strong>Why it won:</strong> it is the only direction where a first-time visitor and a
					returning one both get what they came for in the same screen. Comprehension and mobile
					usability are its strongest scores, and those are the two that decide whether the rest of
					the library is ever seen.
				</p>
				<p>
					<strong>Its weakness</strong> is organic search depth: a question field links to fewer
					substantive pages than a full atlas does. That is why it does not ship pure.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="band band-tight" aria-labelledby="b-title">
		<div class="shell">
			<span class="eyebrow">Direction B, not implemented</span>
			<h2 id="b-title">Evidence Ledger</h2>
			<div class="prose">
				<p>
					Provenance first. The first viewport leads with a direct-answer preview, the source
					registry, review dates, and correction status: the strongest possible trust signal before
					anything else.
				</p>
				<p>
					<strong>Why it did not ship:</strong> it answers a question the visitor has not asked yet.
					Someone arriving with an insurance problem wants the answer; the evidence is what makes
					them believe it, not what makes them start. On mobile it is worse still, because the
					registry consumes the entire first screen before the search field appears.
				</p>
				<p>
					<strong>What was taken from it:</strong> the source-quality strip. It sits inside the first
					viewport of the shipped homepage, counted live from the registry, so the trust signal
					arrives without displacing the working surface.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="band band-tight band-paper" aria-labelledby="c-title">
		<div class="shell">
			<span class="eyebrow">Direction C, not implemented</span>
			<h2 id="c-title">Insurance Topic Atlas</h2>
			<div class="prose">
				<p>
					Browse first. High-scan entry points for coverage, states, companies, questions, and tools
					arranged around a restrained central search field.
				</p>
				<p>
					<strong>Why it did not ship:</strong> an atlas is the right shape for a library that is
					already large. With twelve questions and six coverage pages it advertises its own
					thinness, and it makes the visitor choose a category before they have expressed a need,
					which is the wrong order for someone who does not yet know which category their problem
					belongs to.
				</p>
				<p>
					<strong>What was taken from it:</strong> the entry-point grid, moved below the fold on the
					shipped homepage with live counts on each tile. It gives the internal-linking depth that
					direction A lacked, without making the first screen a menu.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="band band-ink">
		<div class="shell">
			<span class="eyebrow">Decision</span>
			<h2>A, with grafts from B and C</h2>
			<p class="lede">
				The shipped homepage is direction A's spine: identity, scope, question field, context
				controls, browse fallback. Direction B's source-quality strip sits inside the first viewport.
				Direction C's entry-point grid sits below it with live counts. Directions B and C are
				preserved here as alternatives and are not reachable from the live site.
			</p>
			<p class="lede" style="margin-top: var(--s-4)">
				Recorded in full in <code>DESIGN-REFERENCES.md</code>, which is the source of truth for this
				decision.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>
</BaseLayout>
